{"content":[{"c":["HUSHAH, HUSHATHITES*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Ezer’s son (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.4.4","r":"1Chr.4.4","rawR":"1Chr.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.4.4","value":"1Chr.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 4:4"},") or perhaps a town that Ezer founded. The warriors Sibbecai (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.21.18","r":"2Sam.21.18","rawR":"2Sam.21.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.21.18","value":"2Sam.21.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 21:18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.11.29","r":"1Chr.11.29","rawR":"1Chr.11.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.11.29","value":"1Chr.11.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 11:29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.20.4","r":"1Chr.20.4","rawR":"1Chr.20.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.20.4","value":"1Chr.20.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"20:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.27.11","r":"1Chr.27.11","rawR":"1Chr.27.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.27.11","value":"1Chr.27.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"27:11"},") and Mebunnai (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.23.27","r":"2Sam.23.27","rawR":"2Sam.23.27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.23.27","value":"2Sam.23.27"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 23:27"},") were described as Hushathites. Whether this designates genealogical ancestry or geographical locality (or perhaps both) is uncertain."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"HushahHushathites","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"1 Chr 4:4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Chr.4.4","rawQueryValue":"1Chr.4.4","sourceRefExact":"1Chr.4.4"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 21:18","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.21.18","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.21.18","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.21.18"},{"anchorText":"1 Chr 11:29","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Chr.11.29","rawQueryValue":"1Chr.11.29","sourceRefExact":"1Chr.11.29"},{"anchorText":"20:4","canonicalizati